Time to wake {{ robot.name }} up and get it onto your Wi-Fi. You only do this
once — the robot remembers your network afterwards.

## 1. Join the robot's setup hotspot

Flip the power switch on. On first boot the robot opens its own Wi-Fi hotspot
named `Botforge-XXXX` (the XXXX is unique to your robot). Join it from your
phone or laptop — a **captive portal** setup page pops up automatically, like
hotel Wi-Fi. If it doesn't appear, open a browser and go to any http page.

## 2. Pick your Wi-Fi and name your robot

In the portal, choose your home Wi-Fi network, enter its password, and give
your robot a name. The name becomes its address on your network, so keep it
simple — lowercase, no spaces. The portal also shows the robot's IP address
and a QR code once it connects: **note the IP down**, it's handy later.

## 3. Say hello

Once the robot joins your network, open:

```
http://<name>.local
```

…replacing `<name>` with the name you chose.

:::note[iPhone or iPad?]
iOS is picky about `.local` (mDNS) addresses and sometimes refuses to resolve
them. If the page won't load, use the **IP address shown in the setup portal**
instead — e.g. `http://192.168.1.42`.
:::

## 4. Take it for a spin

Head to the [/drive](/drive/) page on the site, connect to your robot, and
drive it around with the on-screen joystick. Driving stops instantly when you
let go — that's the built-in deadman safety.

## Factory reset

Robot moving to a new Wi-Fi network, or typo in the password? Hold the
**BOOT** button for **5 seconds** while the robot is on. It forgets its Wi-Fi
settings and returns to the `Botforge-XXXX` setup hotspot, ready for step 1
again.

All good? Go teach it some tricks on the [play page](./play/).
